  "query_preview": "..Today through Thursday...Heights begin to rise over\nArizona as high pressure centered over the Mississippi River \nValley begins to retrograde back over the southwest. Gradually \nwarming temperatures are expected across northern Arizona as a \nresult, with highs around 5-10F above normal by the middle of the \nweek.\n\nMoisture looks to remain trapped under the ridge, with PWATs \nremaining around 0.75 to 1.00 inches across northern Arizona. \nTherefore, at least isolated afternoon shower and thunderstorm \nactivity looks to remain a possibility over the coming days. Any \nactivity that does develop will be fairly minor, and isolated in \nnature, as warming aloft will inhibit any substantial convective \ndevelopment. The best chances for any activity look to mainly be \nacross the eastern Mogollon Rim into the White Mountains, and \npotentially the Chuska Mountains. However, coverage should gradually \nextend westward some through the week as the subsidence aloft \nlessens.\n\nFriday through Sunday...Later in the week, the details in the \nforecast get a little fuzzy. The high over the southwest remains \nfairly transient, once again moving back eastward and setting up \nover New Mexico. At the same time, a strong trough looks to move \ninto the Pacific Northwest. Breezier, and cooler conditions look to \nset in across northern Arizona over the weekend as the ridge loosens \nit's grip.\n\nThe tricky part here results from uncertainty in how far south the \ntrough may go. A further southward track into the Great Basin would \nresult in a day of active monsoonal conditions, before eventually \nsuppressing monsoonal moisture southward. If it were to track further \nnorth across northern Rockies, the high looks to remain in a \nfavorable monsoonal pattern, thus keeping active conditions into \nthe weekend. Much of the GEFS members seem to favor keeping a more \nactive pattern in place, while the EPS and CMCE both seem to favor a \nstronger influence from the trough.",
